<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Childir Eyalet</h1><p class="st-meta">1628 CE – 1829 CE · capital Akhaltsikhe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Ottoman province of Akhaltsikhe in the south-west Caucasus, governed by Islamised Georgian pashas for two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After decades of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-safavid-wars">Ottoman-Safavid wars</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> turned the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-samtskhe">Principality of Samtskhe</a> into a province in 1628. Its first governor was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safar-pasha">Safar Pasha</a>, a member of the Georgian Jaqeli family who had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and the post remained largely hereditary in his house.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhaltsikhe">Akhaltsikhe</a>, the eyalet included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardahan">Ardahan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artvin">Artvin</a> and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kura-river">Kura</a> valleys. Much of the population, Georgian-speaking Meskhetians, gradually adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, while Armenian and Jewish merchants lived in the towns. Akhaltsikhe had a notorious slave market fed by raids into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a>. Its pashas, often semi-independent, intervened in the politics of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kartli">Kartli</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Imereti</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1828-1829">Russo-Turkish War</a> Russian forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-paskevich">Paskevich</a> stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhaltsikhe">Akhaltsikhe</a> in 1828, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-adrianople-1829">Treaty of Adrianople</a> in 1829 gave the northern part to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. The remainder stayed Ottoman until 1878. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meskhetian-turks">Meskhetian Turks</a> descend from its Muslim population.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/childir-eyalet.jpg" alt="Childir Eyalet" width="320" height="250"><figcaption>Image: Underlying lk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cildir_Eyalet,_Ottoman_Empire_(1609).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1628 CE – 1829 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Akhaltsikhe</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Safar Pasha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam; Georgian and Armenian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Georgian; Ottoman Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>, <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pashalik of Akhaltsikhe, Akhaltsikhe Pashalik</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1728&amp;focus=childir-eyalet" aria-label="Childir Eyalet on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5734 710 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Childir Eyalet lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6054 966L6047 950M6054 966L5709 970M6054 966L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6047 950h0M5709 970h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6054 966h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6054 966h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1728&amp;focus=childir-eyalet">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
